# Objective

The objective of this room was to become familiar with SQL injection, understand how SQL queries and syntax work, and learn how user-controlled input can manipulate vulnerable SQL queries.

The room also introduced different SQL injection techniques and demonstrated how SQL syntax can be used during authorized web application security testing to enumerate database information and understand how vulnerable applications interact with their databases.

---

# Why SQL Injection Matters

Web applications frequently interact with databases to store and retrieve information.

This can include:

- User accounts
- Password-related information
- Products
- Orders
- Application data
- Administrative information

If user-controlled input is handled insecurely before being included in a SQL query, an attacker may be able to change the intended behavior of that query.

```text
User Input
    |
    v
Web Application
    |
    v
SQL Query
    |
    v
Database
    |
    v
Results
```

In a vulnerable application:

```text
Attacker-Controlled Input
          |
          v
Vulnerable Application
          |
          v
SQL Query Is Altered
          |
          v
Database Executes Modified Query
          |
          v
Unintended Results
```

This can potentially expose sensitive information or cause other unintended database actions.

